{
  "brief_rationale": "The doctor performed core neonatal vomiting risk stratification, repeatedly escalated for major red flags, reconciled vitamin AD/D after caregiver label/pharmacy confirmation, and guided follow-up with neonatal service. However, there were responsibility-chain gaps: the vomiting red-flag screen/return precautions never explicitly covered bloody or coffee-ground emesis, discharge diagnoses such as hemangioma/PFO were not safely handed off, and the family plan did not include a true twin-specific separation system or requested teach-back. These are minor/moderate in-chain errors rather than a serious unsafe error because the infant’s observed emesis was repeatedly white, non-projectile, with stable systemic signs and a next-day neonatal appointment. Under the rubric, any minor/moderate responsibility-chain error caps the trajectory at 2 despite some strong communication and repair work.",
